{"parentUuid":"d695d4c1-6d42-4228-856a-2cff0276bcf8","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d2d8c1c5-cf59-474f-8fab-14abba897913","agentId":"a08387fbca2c9ad18","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01P6VsJmf9c7zf2QAohSDmWi","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"OR-Tools CP-SAT clause_cleanup_period parameter memory tuning\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"or-tools/ortools/sat/sat_parameters.proto at stable · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/blob/stable/ortools/sat/sat_parameters.proto\"},{\"title\":\"Parameters - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/05_parameters.html\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - d-krupke/cpsat-primer: The CP-SAT Primer: Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/d-krupke/cpsat-primer\"},{\"title\":\"CP-SAT Solver | OR-Tools | Google for Developers\",\"url\":\"https://developers.google.com/optimization/cp/cp_solver\"},{\"title\":\"Memory leak on CP SAT solver · Issue #3212 · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/issues/3212\"},{\"title\":\"CP-Sat: Python 3.7.2, ortools 7.0 wrong optimization result · Issue #1162 · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/issues/1162\"},{\"title\":\"ortools.sat.python.cp_model API documentation\",\"url\":\"https://or-tools.github.io/docs/pdoc/ortools/sat/python/cp_model.html\"},{\"title\":\"OR-Tools Laurent Perron (lperron@google.com)\",\"url\":\"https://egon.cheme.cmu.edu/ewo/docs/CP-SAT%20and%20OR-Tools.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"CP-SAT: Performance drops from 60s to 700s - why? · Issue #1102 · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/issues/1102\"},{\"title\":\"Introduction - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found about the `clause_cleanup_period` parameter in OR-Tools CP-SAT:\n\nThe `clause_cleanup_period` parameter has a default value of 10000. This parameter is part of the SAT solver's clause management system.\n\nThere is also a `clause_cleanup_period_increment` parameter (default value 0) that increases the `clause_cleanup_period` by a specified amount after each cleanup.\n\nRelated to memory management, the solver has a `clause_cleanup_target` parameter that, when set to 0, deletes the `clause_cleanup_ratio` of \"deletable\" clauses instead of aiming for a fixed target of clauses to keep. Additionally, the `clause_cleanup_ratio` parameter (default 0.5) helps determine which clauses with a low literal blocks distance (LBD) are kept.\n\nThese parameters work together to control how often and how aggressively the solver cleans up clauses during the search, which directly impacts memory usage. Adjusting `clause_cleanup_period` can help tune memory consumption—increasing it means less frequent cleanups (potentially using more memory), while decreasing it means more frequent cleanups (potentially using less memory but with more computational overhead).\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"39b98c80-957f-41f2-9b9e-c60caca1c116","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:51:16.279Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"d695d4c1-6d42-4228-856a-2cff0276bcf8","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"7d6e9acb-2d3e-4650-bd78-36611e5e20ac","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d2d8c1c5-cf59-474f-8fab-14abba897913","agentId":"a08387fbca2c9ad18","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WjM4QQXdx3eSzDPqf9XhL7","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"CP-SAT OR-Tools memory consumption learned clauses internal data structures\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"GitHub - d-krupke/cpsat-primer: The CP-SAT Primer: Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/d-krupke/cpsat-primer\"},{\"title\":\"OR-Tools Laurent Perron (lperron@google.com)\",\"url\":\"https://egon.cheme.cmu.edu/ewo/docs/CP-SAT%20and%20OR-Tools.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"CP-SAT Solver | OR-Tools | Google for Developers\",\"url\":\"https://developers.google.com/optimization/cp/cp_solver\"},{\"title\":\"Cp-Sat python AddAbsEquality drastically increases memory consumption after some time · Issue #2048 · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/issues/2048\"},{\"title\":\"Introduction - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/\"},{\"title\":\"Understanding CP-SAT: Constraint Programming Meets SAT Solving | by Jonas Poelmans | Medium\",\"url\":\"https://medium.com/@jonaspoelmans120/understanding-cp-sat-constraint-programming-meets-sat-solving-b137dddb943c\"},{\"title\":\"How does it work? - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/07_under_the_hood.html\"},{\"title\":\"Basic Modeling - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/04_modelling.html\"},{\"title\":\"Enhancing Hybrid CP-SAT Search for Disjunctive Scheduling\",\"url\":\"https://hal.science/hal-04174800/document\"},{\"title\":\"ortools.sat.python.cp_model API documentation\",\"url\":\"https://or-tools.github.io/docs/pdoc/ortools/sat/python/cp_model.html\"}]\n\nI found search results related to CP-SAT and OR-Tools. Here's what the results reveal about memory consumption, learned clauses, and internal data structures:\n\n## Learned Clauses and Memory\n\nOver the course of solving, the solver might learn thousands or millions of clauses. Clause Learning is critical as it offsets the cost of running the simplex.\n\nConflict-driven clause learning (CDCL) is the key technique that makes SAT solving efficient. When the solver hits a dead end, it analyzes why the conflict occurred and learns a new clause preventing similar conflicts. The analysis produces a learned clause that is added to the problem permanently for the duration of the solve.\n\n## Memory Concerns with Internal Representations\n\nA key technical consideration regarding memory consumption comes from the literal representation used in CP-SAT: Increasing literal size from 32 bits to 64 bits might negatively impact memory consumption and cache usage.\n\n## Overall Architecture\n\nCP-SAT is a versatile portfolio solver centered around a Lazy Clause Generation (LCG) based Constraint Programming Solver. In its role as a portfolio solver, CP-SAT concurrently executes a multitude of diverse algorithms and strategies, each possessing unique strengths and weaknesses. These elements operate largely independently but engage in information exchange, sharing progress when better solutions emerge or tighter bounds become available.\n\nThe search results indicate that while clause learning is essential to solver performance, the accumulation of learned clauses and the literal representation choices directly impact memory consumption.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"e2a92e36-4590-4267-9114-a1931b14b9ca","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:51:17.473Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"7d6e9acb-2d3e-4650-bd78-36611e5e20ac","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"0749daed-fc29-4997-ad05-9327526f51dc","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d2d8c1c5-cf59-474f-8fab-14abba897913","agentId":"a08387fbca2c9ad18","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VLLuWVUh6onXqYPc6BWNwg","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"CP-SAT num_search_workers memory per worker independent state\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"CP-SAT Solver | OR-Tools | Google for Developers\",\"url\":\"https://developers.google.com/optimization/cp/cp_solver\"},{\"title\":\"Parameters - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/05_parameters.html\"},{\"title\":\"CP-SAT: Higher num_search_workers value increases the resolution time · Issue #1921 · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/issues/1921\"},{\"title\":\"CP-SAT solver couldn't prove the solution is optimal if num_workers < 5 but otherwise it did instantly. · Issue #3662 · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/issues/3662\"},{\"title\":\"Get nondeterministic results with different number of workers using CP-SAT; Optimal using 1 worker, Infeasible using 8 workers · Issue #3842 · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/issues/3842\"},{\"title\":\"Find many CP-SAT solutions, multi-threaded, under time constraint\",\"url\":\"https://groups.google.com/g/or-tools-discuss/c/GNgvcMpEwmE\"},{\"title\":\"Seeding CP-SAT solver\",\"url\":\"https://groups.google.com/g/or-tools-discuss/c/lPb1FzhTMt0\"},{\"title\":\"Parallelism and multi threads\",\"url\":\"https://groups.google.com/g/or-tools-discuss/c/n1pAIfAQs7I\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - d-krupke/cpsat-primer: The CP-SAT Primer: Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/d-krupke/cpsat-primer\"},{\"title\":\"Solver Max - Well, that escalated quickly: OR-Tools\",\"url\":\"https://www.solvermax.com/blog/well-that-escalated-quickly-or-tools\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found relevant information about CP-SAT's `num_search_workers` parameter:\n\n## Key Information about CP-SAT num_search_workers\n\n**Portfolio-Based Parallelization:**\nCP-SAT is a portfolio-solver that uses different techniques to solve the problem, with some information exchange between workers but without splitting the solution space into different parts like MIP-solvers do.\n\n**Worker Independence and Memory:**\nThe search results indicate that workers do not parallelize the branch-and-bound algorithm by splitting the solution space into different parts, meaning each worker maintains an independent state. However, the search results do not contain specific information about memory allocation per worker.\n\n**Deterministic Behavior with Independent State:**\nTo be deterministic, the solve is split into batch size independent chunks, which are waited for to be solved by the workers, then synchronized to generate the next batch. This indicates that workers operate with independent states during each batch.\n\n**Practical Considerations:**\nBy default, CP-SAT leverages all available cores (including hyperthreading), and you can control the parallelization of CP-SAT by setting the number of search workers.\n\nThe search results don't provide specific details about memory allocation per worker, but they do confirm that workers maintain independent search states within the portfolio approach.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"2727a766-2cda-4f08-8e7c-3fd29fb470de","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:51:17.639Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"0749daed-fc29-4997-ad05-9327526f51dc","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"33796b76-e7ab-44bf-8f3a-839a6a182486","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d2d8c1c5-cf59-474f-8fab-14abba897913","agentId":"a08387fbca2c9ad18","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016jcTE37MaXDJ9Kqy4yXQVg","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"SAT solver memory profile clause database watched literals percentage breakdown\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"memory layout | Wonderings of a SAT geek\",\"url\":\"https://www.msoos.org/tag/memory-layout/\"},{\"title\":\"Modern SAT solvers: fast, neat and underused (part 3 of N) — The Coding Nest\",\"url\":\"https://codingnest.com/modern-sat-solvers-fast-neat-and-underused-part-3-of-n/\"},{\"title\":\"Watched Literals in SAT and CP\",\"url\":\"https://school.a4cp.org/summer2011/slides/Gent/SATCP3.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"SAT Solver Descriptions: CMUSAT-Base and CMUSAT Himanshu Jain\",\"url\":\"https://www.cs.cmu.edu/~hjain/papers/cmusat-solvers.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"SAT solver - Wikipedia\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SAT_solver\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - radekm/satviz: SAT solver visualization. Supports watched literals and learned clause minimization.\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/radekm/satviz\"},{\"title\":\"Scalable Proof Producing Multi-Threaded SAT Solving with ...\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2207.13577\"},{\"title\":\"PicoSAT Essentials - Armin Biere, 2008\",\"url\":\"https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/abs/10.3233/SAT190039\"},{\"title\":\"Cache Performance of SAT Solvers: A Case Study for Eﬃcient Implementation of\",\"url\":\"https://www.ccs.neu.edu/home/lieber/courses/csg260/f06/materials/papers/sat/SAT_2003_cache.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"A Verified SAT Solver with Watched Literals Using Imperative HOL Mathias Fleury\",\"url\":\"https://people.mpi-inf.mpg.de/~mfleury/sat_twl.pdf\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found relevant information about SAT solver memory profiles related to clause databases and watched literals. Here are the key findings:\n\n## Memory Profile Breakdown\n\nIf a SAT solver uses the typical watched literal scheme, it needs 2 arrays for each variable, and with 64-bit pointers and 32-bit array sizes, that's 32 bytes for each variable, or 32MB for every million variables. For a 100 million variable problem, this amounts to 3.2GB of memory only to hold the watching literal array pointers and sizes, not including any actual clause data.\n\n## Clause Database Optimization\n\nIn typical CNF problems, the majority of clauses are binary (containing only 2 literals), and these clauses are traditionally stored in the heap with pointers placed in the watchlist. However, an improvement uses implicit clauses, where for a binary clause \"x V y\", the literal y is placed in the watchlist of x and x in the watchlist of y, with no other storage location needed for these binary clauses.\n\n## Cache Performance Benefits\n\nOne optimization involves copying watched literals into a separate location to decrease cache pressure, since when a clause is examined due to a watch, the most common result is that the clause is satisfied, usually due to the other watched literal.\n\n## Overall Impact\n\nOptimized compact data structures for watching literals not only save memory but also speed up SAT solvers considerably.\n\nThe search results don't contain specific percentage breakdowns of memory distribution between clause database and watched literals components, but they highlight that watched literal structures represent a significant portion of overall memory usage.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"661db2b4-0b26-4d86-bae7-f90a2acd2c77","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:51:18.535Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"33796b76-e7ab-44bf-8f3a-839a6a182486","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
