{"parentUuid":"1d3f4944-3283-4c3c-b5cd-d1615b76f365","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a08387fbca2c9ad18","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01XUztymZ1emL5kCf6tXDPf2","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01KpwpwEeJRcpjqReAHS4AUw","name":"WebSearch","input":{"query":"Windows VMMap process memory commit private bytes mmap analysis"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2446,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2991,"cache_read_input_tokens":21262,"output_tokens":299,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2991},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2446,"output_tokens":299,"cache_read_input_tokens":21262,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2991,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2991,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"}},"requestId":"req_011CaosQ8pTCh9sbFSgt1bdM","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"200bf129-7e63-4b79-bee5-4609c6543801","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:51:23.686Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"987d0be4-3254-4d41-aa12-21b8e533fce0","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d2d8c1c5-cf59-474f-8fab-14abba897913","agentId":"a08387fbca2c9ad18","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SfawKTNSpV8XnX4Lzqdi1B","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"OR-Tools CP-SAT memory leak issue 3212 RSS growth large model\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Memory leak on CP SAT solver · Issue #3212 · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/issues/3212\"},{\"title\":\"Possible Memory Leak · Issue #2782 · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/issues/2782\"},{\"title\":\"Memory Leak · Issue #3083 · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/issues/3083\"},{\"title\":\"Valgrind reports leak on simple CPSAT example · Issue #3961 · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/issues/3961\"},{\"title\":\"[java] RoutingModel: memory leak · Issue #3446 · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/issues/3446\"},{\"title\":\"Cp-Sat python AddAbsEquality drastically increases memory consumption after some time · Issue #2048 · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/issues/2048\"},{\"title\":\"How to investigate RSS memory leak? · Issue #4141 · nodejs/help\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/nodejs/help/issues/4141\"},{\"title\":\"RSS keeps increasing...Memory leaks??\",\"url\":\"https://groups.google.com/g/comp.unix.solaris/c/Ed_hGn4-Eto\"},{\"title\":\"CP-SAT multi-thread crashing python w/ large number of workers · Issue #2019 · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/issues/2019\"},{\"title\":\"Multithreading: solving different problems with CP-SAT ends in random crash · Issue #1958 · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/issues/1958\"}]\n\nBased on the web search results, I found a GitHub issue #3212 about a memory leak in the CP-SAT solver from OR-Tools v9.2 in C++.\n\nThe search results show several related memory leak issues in OR-Tools, including:\n\n- A report where the CP-SAT solver showed consistently increasing memory usage until the process was killed (Linux) or stalled (Windows)\n\n- One user reported that reverting to ortools v9.0.9048 resolved the memory issue\n\nHowever, the search results don't provide the detailed discussion or resolution from issue #3212 itself. To get more specific information about the RSS growth issue with large models in that particular GitHub issue, you would need to visit the full issue page at https://github.com/google/or-tools/issues/3212 directly.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"fb233fac-7d9a-46ab-a6a6-0e83415a7faa","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:51:26.493Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"987d0be4-3254-4d41-aa12-21b8e533fce0","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"8c8c5b1e-fd68-4ccf-9f21-eae1c2131b90","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d2d8c1c5-cf59-474f-8fab-14abba897913","agentId":"a08387fbca2c9ad18","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MWPkJG5bQfGz4UGJ7gtSBA","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"CP-SAT presolve memory peak inprocessing temporary doubling\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"SAT Presolve time limit · google/or-tools · Discussion #4280\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/discussions/4280\"},{\"title\":\"CP-SAT solver takes way too long to shut down or does not stop at all despite given time limit · Issue #4882 · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/issues/4882\"},{\"title\":\"or-tools/ortools/sat/cp_model_presolve.cc at stable · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/blob/stable/ortools/sat/cp_model_presolve.cc\"},{\"title\":\"[CP-SAT] Ortools stops when solving a RCPSP - Memory exhausted?\",\"url\":\"https://groups.google.com/g/or-tools-discuss/c/d-3P1dq-RBY\"},{\"title\":\"Parameters - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/05_parameters.html\"},{\"title\":\"or-tools/ortools/sat/sat_parameters.proto at stable · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/blob/stable/ortools/sat/sat_parameters.proto\"},{\"title\":\"Memory Efficient Parallel SAT Solving with Inprocessing | IEEE Conference Publication | IEEE Xplore\",\"url\":\"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/8995355/\"},{\"title\":\"Certified SAT solving with GPU accelerated inprocessing | Formal Methods in System Design | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10703-023-00432-z\"},{\"title\":\"trying to solve huge mode on CP-Sat\",\"url\":\"https://groups.google.com/g/or-tools-discuss/c/VjiSYjFFB_4\"},{\"title\":\"dblp: Memory Efficient Parallel SAT Solving with Inprocessing.\",\"url\":\"https://dblp.org/rec/conf/ictai/IserBS19.html\"}]\n\nI found relevant search results about CP-SAT presolve, memory, and inprocessing. Here's what I found:\n\n## Key Findings:\n\n**Inprocessing in CP-SAT:** Inprocessing is SAT presolving done at each restart to the root level. There's a parameter to control the proportion of deterministic time spent on inprocessing, and if the proportion is below a certain ratio, inprocessing will be performed at each restart.\n\n**Memory Issues with CP-SAT:** The search results show several discussions about memory-related problems with CP-SAT presolve. Users have reported issues where the CP-SAT solver doesn't stop despite time limits and runs until memory overload occurs. Additionally, there are indications that newer versions of OR-Tools require significantly more memory than earlier versions.\n\n**Inprocessing Parameters:** There are parameters controlling the amount of deterministic time spent on probing for each inprocessing round, which can help manage memory usage during inprocessing operations.\n\n**General SAT Inprocessing Memory:** In the broader SAT solver context, research shows that parallel portfolio SAT solvers can suffer from high memory consumption when performing inprocessing with shared clause databases.\n\nThe search results discuss memory peaks during presolve and inprocessing, though I didn't find specific documentation about \"temporary doubling\" of memory. This phrase may refer to temporary memory allocation patterns during inprocessing operations, but the exact mechanism isn't detailed in these results.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"80e2156e-b7de-4c44-a004-7f5555f84d06","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:51:28.947Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"8c8c5b1e-fd68-4ccf-9f21-eae1c2131b90","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"1d3f4944-3283-4c3c-b5cd-d1615b76f365","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d2d8c1c5-cf59-474f-8fab-14abba897913","agentId":"a08387fbca2c9ad18","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GJ7Ro4Lja4BWco69rFiUaf","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"tracemalloc memray ortools C++ extension memory profiling pybind11\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"GitHub - bloomberg/memray: Memray is a memory profiler for Python · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/bloomberg/memray\"},{\"title\":\"Memray: the endgame memory profiler\",\"url\":\"https://bloomberg.github.io/memray/\"},{\"title\":\"Episode #425 - Memray: The endgame Python memory profiler | Talk Python To Me Podcast\",\"url\":\"https://talkpython.fm/episodes/show/425/memray-the-endgame-python-memory-profiler\"},{\"title\":\"Heap memory usage lower than resident size. How to track the leaks ? · bloomberg/memray · Discussion #331\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/bloomberg/memray/discussions/331\"},{\"title\":\"how memray works？ · bloomberg/memray · Discussion #225\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/bloomberg/memray/discussions/225\"},{\"title\":\"Introduction to Memory Profiling in Python | DataCamp\",\"url\":\"https://www.datacamp.com/tutorial/memory-profiling-python\"},{\"title\":\"Bloomberg Open-Sources Python Memory Profiler Memray - InfoQ\",\"url\":\"https://www.infoq.com/news/2022/04/bloomberg-memray-python-profiler/\"},{\"title\":\"memray · PyPI\",\"url\":\"https://pypi.org/project/memray/\"},{\"title\":\"Where Did All the RAM Go? Memory Profiling with Memray | McGinnis, Will\",\"url\":\"https://mcginniscommawill.com/posts/2025-03-01-testing-profiling-memray/\"},{\"title\":\"Bloomberg Open-Sources Memray: A Memory Profiler That Can Track Memory Allocations In Python Code In Native Extension Modules And Python Interpreter - MarkTechPost\",\"url\":\"https://www.marktechpost.com/2022/04/30/bloomberg-open-sources-memray-a-memory-profiler-that-can-track-memory-allocations-in-python-code-in-native-extension-modules-and-python-interpreter/\"}]\n\nI've completed the web search for your query about tracemalloc, memray, ortools, C++ extension memory profiling, and pybind11. Here are the key findings:\n\n## Memory Profiling Tools Overview\n\nMemray is a memory profiler for Python that can track memory allocations in Python code, in native extension modules, and in the Python interpreter itself. It tracks and reports memory allocations, both in Python code and in compiled extension modules.\n\n### Memray's Capabilities with Native Code\n\nMemray handles native calls in C/C++ libraries so the entire call stack is present in the results. This is particularly relevant for projects using pybind11 or other C++ binding frameworks. Memray can see into compiled component layers to help diagnose hidden memory usage that pure Python profilers might miss, which is especially helpful for data science workloads that offload computations to C/C++ or Rust.\n\n### How Memray Works with Extensions\n\nMemray captures information about allocations by detecting when memory is allocated from the system or deallocated (via malloc, free, realloc, etc), recording the Python stack associated with each allocation, and optionally recording the native stack associated with those allocations.\n\n### Comparison with tracemalloc\n\nTracemalloc is a powerful built-in module in Python that tracks memory allocations and deallocations, providing an easy-to-use interface for capturing and analyzing memory usage snapshots. However, unlike Memray, tracemalloc is primarily designed for Python-level profiling and doesn't have built-in capabilities to track C++ extension memory as comprehensively.\n\n### Platform Availability\n\nMemray only works on Linux and MacOS, and cannot be installed on other platforms.\n\nThe search results don't contain specific information about OR-Tools integration with these profiling tools, but the general approach would be to use Memray's native code profiling capabilities to analyze memory usage in OR-Tools' C++ components when called from Python bindings.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"36bd0cd7-cdfb-4e59-adcd-e774cff68f44","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:51:30.910Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"1d3f4944-3283-4c3c-b5cd-d1615b76f365","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"200bf129-7e63-4b79-bee5-4609c6543801","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d2d8c1c5-cf59-474f-8fab-14abba897913","agentId":"a08387fbca2c9ad18","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KpwpwEeJRcpjqReAHS4AUw","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Windows VMMap process memory commit private bytes mmap analysis\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Windows Process Memory Usage Demystified\",\"url\":\"https://dzone.com/articles/windows-process-memory-usage-demystified\"},{\"title\":\"VMMap - Sysinternals | Microsoft Learn\",\"url\":\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sysinternals/downloads/vmmap\"},{\"title\":\"Using VMMap - Documentation & Help\",\"url\":\"https://documentation.help/VMMap/Using_VMMap.htm\"},{\"title\":\"Private bytes - Microsoft Q&A\",\"url\":\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/answers/questions/1017628/private-bytes\"},{\"title\":\"VMMap Copyright © 2009-2010 Mark Russinovich and Bryce Cogswell\",\"url\":\"https://documentation.help/VMMap/documentation.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - Alois-xx/MemAnalyzer: A command line memory analysis tool for managed code.\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/Alois-xx/MemAnalyzer\"},{\"title\":\"System Committed Memory - an overview | ScienceDirect Topics\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/system-committed-memory\"},{\"title\":\"Mysteries of Windows Memory Management Revealed - ppt download\",\"url\":\"https://slideplayer.com/slide/12841421/\"},{\"title\":\"Using RamMap and VMMap Tools to Troubleshoot Windows Memory Issues | TechTarget\",\"url\":\"https://www.techtarget.com/searchwindowsserver/tip/Using-RamMap-and-VMMap-Tools-to-Troubleshoot-Windows-Memory-Issues\"},{\"title\":\"Delving Deep into Windows Memory Management\",\"url\":\"https://www.linkedin.com/pulse/delving-deep-windows-memory-management-brahim-redouane-mellah--uzaee\"}]\n\nI found comprehensive information about Windows VMMap process memory analysis. Here are the key results:\n\n## VMMap Overview\n\nVMMap is a process virtual and physical memory analysis utility that shows a breakdown of a process's committed virtual memory types as well as the amount of physical memory (working set) assigned by the operating system to those types.\n\n## Committed Bytes\n\nCommitted bytes information is available in VMMap under total > committed and the process > page file bytes performance counter. The Commit Summary Graph shows the committed memory usage of the process by type, representing data or code.\n\n## Private Bytes\n\nPrivate bytes refer to memory that is not shared or shareable with other processes, and this information is available in VMMap under total > private. The Private Summary Graph shows committed private virtual memory that is backed by the paging file and charged against the system commit limit, corresponding to the PrivateBytes performance counter.\n\n## Memory Mapping Analysis\n\nVMMap shows detailed breakdown of process address space including private process memory, copy-on-write private memory, heap and GC heap, stack, shareable process memory, images, shareable memory, mapped files, page table, and unusable gaps between allocations.\n\n## Working Set\n\nThe Working Set Summary Graph shows the working set usage of the process by memory type, where working set represents the amount of committed virtual memory that's in physical memory and owned by the process.\n\nThe search results provide detailed technical documentation about how VMMap analyzes process memory, including the relationships between committed memory, private bytes, and memory mapping characteristics.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"366442eb-108f-4624-84a8-0d32c5a9c235","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:51:31.178Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"200bf129-7e63-4b79-bee5-4609c6543801","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
