{"parentUuid":"c266d192-ea39-4833-90ff-2cb54d9bfbd7","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d2d8c1c5-cf59-474f-8fab-14abba897913","agentId":"a08387fbca2c9ad18","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QjHwwiNXx5raoULPohqM6g","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"\"or-tools\" CP-SAT large model memory 30GB 40GB scheduling routing\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"GitHub - d-krupke/cpsat-primer: The CP-SAT Primer: Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/d-krupke/cpsat-primer\"},{\"title\":\"OR-Tools Release Notes | Google for Developers\",\"url\":\"https://developers.google.com/optimization/support/release_notes\"},{\"title\":\"CP-SAT for scheduling Laurent Perron (lperron@google.com)\",\"url\":\"https://schedulingseminar.com/presentations/SchedulingSeminar_LaurentPerron.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Constraint Optimization | OR-Tools | Google for Developers\",\"url\":\"https://developers.google.com/optimization/cp/\"},{\"title\":\"Advanced Modeling - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/04B_advanced_modelling.html\"},{\"title\":\"CP-SAT Rostering: Complete Guide to Constraint Programming for Workforce Scheduling - Interactive | Michael Brenndoerfer | Michael Brenndoerfer\",\"url\":\"https://mbrenndoerfer.com/writing/cp-sat-rostering-constraint-programming-workforce-scheduling\"},{\"title\":\"Memory usage while using AddElement constraint\",\"url\":\"https://groups.google.com/g/or-tools-discuss/c/enAzF6Cv2vg\"},{\"title\":\"OR-Tools Laurent Perron (lperron@google.com)\",\"url\":\"https://egon.cheme.cmu.edu/ewo/docs/CP-SAT%20and%20OR-Tools.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Introduction - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/\"},{\"title\":\"Cp-Sat python AddAbsEquality drastically increases memory consumption after some time · Issue #2048 · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/issues/2048\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found relevant information about OR-Tools CP-SAT and memory usage with large models. Here are the key findings:\n\n## Memory Usage Issues with CP-SAT\n\nRecent OR-Tools versions have included improvements to reduce memory footprint for large models. However, there are documented cases of significant memory consumption:\n\n**Real-World Examples:**\n- A user reported ~50GB RAM usage when using AddElement constraints with many variables in a scheduling problem\n- Another user experienced memory growth from 7GB to 32GB when using AddAbsEquality constraints, with about 50 AddAbsEqualitys in their model\n\n## Recommendations for Large Models\n\nFor problems with substantial memory requirements, for problems with more than 10,000 variables, consider using sparse representations or chunking strategies to manage memory usage effectively.\n\nAdditionally, problem decomposition for very large instances can be used, such as solving weekly schedules separately or using hierarchical approaches where different departments are optimized independently.\n\n## CP-SAT Applications\n\nCP-SAT is a constraint programming solver that uses SAT (satisfiability) methods, and these constraints are already focused on specific problems, such as routing or scheduling, but very generic and powerful within their domain.\n\nThe search results indicate that while CP-SAT is powerful for scheduling and routing optimization, users working with large models in the 30-50GB memory range should consider optimization strategies like model decomposition, sparse representations, and careful constraint design to manage memory consumption.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"ab1d884b-f71c-4e85-b6b5-7db88b9100f7","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:51:59.268Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"c266d192-ea39-4833-90ff-2cb54d9bfbd7","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"60230114-59b5-43db-a1cd-2f7b05e5b43f","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d2d8c1c5-cf59-474f-8fab-14abba897913","agentId":"a08387fbca2c9ad18","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016PtMFKJyovxbjvVW8dVMfo","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"CDCL SAT solver memory bytes per learned clause average literals length distribution\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Conflict-driven clause learning (CDCL) SAT solvers — CS-E3220: Propositional satisfiability and SAT solvers documentation\",\"url\":\"https://users.aalto.fi/~tjunttil/2020-DP-AUT/notes-sat/cdcl.html\"},{\"title\":\"Conflict-driven clause learning - Wikipedia\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conflict-driven_clause_learning\"},{\"title\":\"Conflict Driven Clause Learning\",\"url\":\"https://cse442-17f.github.io/Conflict-Driven-Clause-Learning/\"},{\"title\":\"Conflict-Driven Clause Learning SAT Solvers - cs.Princeton\",\"url\":\"https://www.cs.princeton.edu/~zkincaid/courses/fall18/readings/SATHandbook-CDCL.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"SAT Solving --- Basis and CDCL Shaowei Cai\",\"url\":\"http://lcs.ios.ac.cn/~caisw/Talks/ConstraintSolving%5B2%5DSATSolvingBasisAndCDCL.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - sgomber/CDCL-SAT: A SAT Solver based on CDCL (Conflict Driven Clause Learning) implemented in python · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/sgomber/CDCL-SAT\"},{\"title\":\"CDCL SAT Solvers & SAT-Based Problem Solving\",\"url\":\"https://cse.usf.edu/~haozheng/teach/cda5416/slides/intro-sat.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - KomaEc/sat: A simple CDCL sat solver · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/KomaEc/sat\"},{\"title\":\"An Effective Learnt Clause Minimization Approach for CDCL SAT Solvers | IJCAI\",\"url\":\"https://www.ijcai.org/proceedings/2017/98\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - z11i/pysat: Simple SAT solver with CDCL implemented in Python\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/z11i/pysat\"}]\n\nI've completed a web search for information about CDCL SAT solver memory usage, learned clauses, and literals. Here's what I found:\n\n## Key Findings:\n\n**Data Structure Representation:**\nEach clause is represented by an array of literals, and literals have different representations depending on being unassigned, assigned value 0 (unsatisfied) or assigned value 1 (satisfied).\n\n**Learned Clause Characteristics:**\nLearnt clauses in CDCL SAT solvers often contain redundant literals, and the decision whether a clause should be deleted is based not only on the number of literals but also on its activity in contributing to conflict making and on the number of decisions taken since its creation.\n\n**Memory Management:**\nKeeping all learned clauses not only leads to high time consumption of the propagation process, but also makes computer memory explode: in worst case, the number of learned clauses can reach O(2^n).\n\nHowever, the search results don't contain specific numerical data about average bytes per learned clause, exact average literal length distribution patterns, or detailed memory profiling metrics for CDCL solvers. For such specific technical benchmarking data, you might want to look for research papers on SAT solver performance analysis or implementation-specific documentation from solvers like MiniSat, CaDiCaL, or Kissat.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"13d0402a-8fe6-40fe-9421-2e869583127b","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:51:59.408Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"60230114-59b5-43db-a1cd-2f7b05e5b43f","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"8edcfda4-9b68-4297-95cc-99683c1ed42b","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d2d8c1c5-cf59-474f-8fab-14abba897913","agentId":"a08387fbca2c9ad18","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LSWMbGckHP1H1VSbyYk5Cy","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"CP-SAT linearization_level LP matrix memory cost integer variable Boolean encoding\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"OR-Tools Laurent Perron (lperron@google.com)\",\"url\":\"https://egon.cheme.cmu.edu/ewo/docs/CP-SAT%20and%20OR-Tools.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - d-krupke/cpsat-primer: The CP-SAT Primer: Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/d-krupke/cpsat-primer\"},{\"title\":\"CP-SAT for scheduling Laurent Perron (lperron@google.com)\",\"url\":\"https://schedulingseminar.com/presentations/SchedulingSeminar_LaurentPerron.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Understanding CP-SAT: Constraint Programming Meets SAT Solving | by Jonas Poelmans | Medium\",\"url\":\"https://medium.com/@jonaspoelmans120/understanding-cp-sat-constraint-programming-meets-sat-solving-b137dddb943c\"},{\"title\":\"Basic Modeling - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/04_modelling.html\"},{\"title\":\"Encoding Linear Constraints into SAT Ignasi Ab´ıo1 and Peter J. Stuckey1,2\",\"url\":\"https://people.eng.unimelb.edu.au/pstuckey/papers/cp2014a.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Selecting SAT Encodings for Pseudo-Boolean and Linear Integer Constraints\",\"url\":\"https://drops.dagstuhl.de/storage/00lipics/lipics-vol235-cp2022/LIPIcs.CP.2022.38/LIPIcs.CP.2022.38.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"How does it work? - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/07_under_the_hood.html\"},{\"title\":\"CP-SAT Solver | OR-Tools | Google for Developers\",\"url\":\"https://developers.google.com/optimization/cp/cp_solver\"},{\"title\":\"Encoding Linear Constraints into SAT | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-319-10428-7_9\"}]\n\nI found search results relevant to your query about CP-SAT linearization_level, LP matrix memory cost, integer variable Boolean encoding. Here are the key findings:\n\n## CP-SAT and Boolean Encoding\n\nCP-SAT uses integer encoding at level 2, where integers are represented as sums of Boolean variables multiplied by values. In CP-SAT, the linear relaxation is on by default.\n\n## Linear Programming Integration\n\nCP-SAT uses LP relaxations for certain constraints (particularly arithmetic constraints over large domains) to compute bounds and prune domains. CP-SAT employs Branch and Cut techniques, including linear relaxations and cutting planes, as part of its toolkit.\n\n## Memory and Performance Considerations\n\nLinear and boolean constraints are generally most efficient, whereas constraints like add_multiplication_equality can be significantly more resource-intensive. CP-SAT lacks support for continuous variables, and in cases where your problem predominantly features continuous variables and linear constraints, opting for an LP-solver is likely to yield significantly improved performance.\n\n## Integer Constraint Encoding\n\nLinear integer constraints are commonly found in practical applications, but encoding linear constraints to SAT typically performs poorly compared to CP or MIP solvers, although some problems with mixed combinatoric and linear constraints can benefit from SAT encoding.\n\nThe search results provide technical documentation on CP-SAT's architecture, but may not contain exhaustive details on the specific `linearization_level` parameter settings and their exact memory cost tradeoffs.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"3450039d-3ee7-404c-9e4d-1ddd0ede94b4","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:51:59.474Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"8edcfda4-9b68-4297-95cc-99683c1ed42b","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"b4ce4b01-becd-4440-bd2b-7fbdfdab4932","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d2d8c1c5-cf59-474f-8fab-14abba897913","agentId":"a08387fbca2c9ad18","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DbguYMddieRsL688YPkram","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"py-spy native memory profiling Linux Windows Python C++ extension RSS\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"GitHub - benfred/py-spy: Sampling profiler for Python programs · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/benfred/py-spy\"},{\"title\":\"Profiling Native Python Extensions - Ben Frederickson\",\"url\":\"https://www.benfrederickson.com/profiling-native-python-extensions-with-py-spy/\"},{\"title\":\"py-spy 0.1.4 - Docs.rs\",\"url\":\"https://docs.rs/crate/py-spy/0.1.4\"},{\"title\":\"py-spy – Low‑overhead Sampling Profiler for Python | PickYourTech\",\"url\":\"https://www.pickyourtech.com/projects/gh-benfred-py-spy\"},{\"title\":\"py-spy | Python Tools – Real Python\",\"url\":\"https://realpython.com/ref/tools/py-spy/\"},{\"title\":\"Spying on Python with py-spy\",\"url\":\"https://codilime.com/blog/spying-on-python-with-py-spy/\"},{\"title\":\"py-spy 0.2.0.dev4\",\"url\":\"https://pypi.org/project/py-spy/0.2.0.dev4/\"},{\"title\":\"py-spy · PyPI\",\"url\":\"https://pypi.org/project/py-spy/0.1.3/\"},{\"title\":\"How to Profile Python Applications with cProfile and py-spy\",\"url\":\"https://oneuptime.com/blog/post/2025-01-06-profile-python-cprofile-pyspy/view\"},{\"title\":\"Python Performance Profiling Using PySpy — Squirro Documentation\",\"url\":\"https://docs.squirro.com/en/latest/technical/admin/troubleshooting/python-profiling-pyspy.html\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found about py-spy and native memory profiling:\n\n## py-spy Overview\n\npy-spy is a Rust-implemented sampling profiler that can attach to any running CPython process without modifying its code. py-spy works by directly reading the memory of the python program using the process_vm_readv system call on Linux, the vm_read call on OSX or the ReadProcessMemory call on Windows.\n\n## Native Extension Profiling\n\npy-spy supports profiling native python extensions written in languages like C/C++ or Cython, on some platforms, and you can enable this mode by passing --native on the command line. To get the native stack, the tool used the StackWalk64 API on Windows and libunwind-ptrace on Linux.\n\n## Platform Support\n\nIt supports profiling native extensions (--native), tracing subprocesses (--subprocesses), and works on Linux, macOS, Windows, and FreeBSD.\n\n## Memory Profiling Limitation\n\nHowever, it's important to note that py-spy is designed specifically for CPU profiling and does not track memory allocation and deallocation. This limitation means it cannot be used to diagnose memory leaks or optimize memory usage. So while py-spy can analyze native code and CPU usage, it does not provide RSS (Resident Set Size) memory profiling capabilities directly. For memory profiling, you would need to use complementary tools like memory_profiler.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"4445f5b9-c6f1-49e0-b510-1c23903a64ae","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:52:00.256Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"b4ce4b01-becd-4440-bd2b-7fbdfdab4932","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
